84375, -0.0198822021484375, 0.006839752197265625, -0.00621795654296875, -0.006801605224609375, 8.189678192138672e-05, 0.01019287109375, 0.0026988983154296875, 0.0019502639770507812, -0.018402099609375, 0.001338958740234375, 0.034454345703125, 0.0199432373046875, 0.0120391845703125, -0.0148162841796875, -0.0013561248779296875, -0.0012693405151367188, 0.01322174072265625, -0.021453857421875, 0.009857177734375, -0.004383087158203125, -0.01367950439453125, 0.0218963623046875, 0.0027179718017578125, 0.0009593963623046875, -0.0004162788391113281, 0.040771484375, 0.025390625, 0.01025390625, -0.01129913330078125, -0.006420135498046875, 0.017974853515625, -0.00531768798828125, 0.005390167236328125, 0.001789093017578125, -0.0138397216796875, -0.0019855499267578125, -0.013336181640625, -0.01557159423828125, -0.00997161865234375, -0.006374359130859375, 0.0190277099609375, -0.053558349609375, 0.017486572265625, -0.00797271728515625, -0.0268707275390625, 0.00656890869140625, -0.02294921875, 0.00994873046875, -0.01318359375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Memory-based self-improving agents\u2014those that learn from an online stream of tasks and improve over time by maintaining a textual memory bank\u2014have shown great promise in recent literature. However, the reliability of these methods\u2014defined here as the consistency and predictability of agent performance across stochastic environments and task orderings\u2014has been critically overlooked. In this work, we address this gap by systematically evaluating the robustness of memory-based self-improving agents and proposing interventions to improve their reliability. We also introduce oversight mechanisms, defined as processes or tools that enable transparent monitoring and auditing of agent memory and behavior.\n\n**1. Introduction and Scope**\n\nRecent advances in memory-based self-improving agents (e.g., Smith et al., 2022; Lee & Gupta, 2023) have demonstrated the potential of textual memory banks for continual learning. However, prior work has primarily focused on average performance, with limited attention to the consistency of results across stochastic factors such as random seeds and task orderings. Our work is the first to (a) systematically quantify robustness and fragility in these agents using variance-based metrics, (b) introduce and empirically validate specification interventions (structured rubrics and environment feedback) for memory construction, (c) propose and evaluate standardized evaluation protocols for reproducibility, and (d) design and assess human-auditable oversight mechanisms for agent memory. Unlike previous studies, we explicitly measure performance variance, analyze memory content quality, and provide transparent, reproducible oversight tools.\n\n**2. Key Terms and Operational Definitions**\n\nTo avoid redundancy, we summarize key terms with single definitions and examples:\n\n| Term         | Definition                                                                 | Example                                                                                   |\n|--------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Reliability  | Consistency and predictability of agent performance across runs and task orderings | An agent consistently achieves similar task completion rates regardless of random seed or task sequence |\n| Robustness   | Stability of agent performance under varying conditions (e.g., random seeds, task order) | Performance variance (e.g., standard deviation) remains low when task order or environment changes |\n| Fragility    | High sensitivity of agent performance to stochasticity or task order, resulting in large performance variance | Agent\u2019s completion rate drops sharply when task order is shuffled or environment is perturbed |\n\n**3. Main Contributions and Novelty**\n\nOur main contributions, and how they advance beyond prior work, are as follows:\n\n1. **Systematic Robustness Evaluation:**  \n   - We provide the first comprehensive quantification of robustness and fragility in memory-based self-improving agents by measuring performance variance (standard deviation, confidence intervals) across multiple independent runs and randomized task orderings. Prior work (Smith et al., 2022; Lee & Gupta, 2023) reported only mean performance, without variance or sensitivity analyses.\n\n2. **Specification Interventions:**  \n   - We introduce automated integration of structured rubrics and explicit environment feedback into agent memory entries. Unlike previous studies, which relied on unstructured or loosely formatted memory, our interventions use template-based extraction and rule-based parsing to standardize and enrich memory content, directly targeting sources of fragility.\n\n3. **Standardized Evaluation Protocols:**  \n   - We propose and empirically validate protocols requiring results to be reported across multiple runs and randomized curricula, using standardized benchmarks. This addresses reproducibility gaps in prior literature, where single-seed or fixed-order evaluations were common.\n\n4. **Oversight Mechanisms:**  \n   - We design and evaluate a human-auditable memory interface, enabling external reviewers to inspect, annotate, and edit agent memory. This interface provides features for filtering, searching, and annotating memory entries, and supports collaborative review. Prior work did not provide such tools or quantitative evaluation of oversight.\n\n**4. Background and Methodological Details**\n\nWe focus on two representative memory-based methods:\n\n- **Episodic Memory Augmented Agents (EMAA):** Agents store and retrieve textual summaries of past task experiences to inform future decisions. After each task, EMAA encodes a summary of the episode (e.g., actions taken, outcomes, and lessons learned) into a memory bank, which is then queried during subsequent tasks to guide behavior.\n- **Self-Reflective Memory Agents (SRMA):** Agents maintain a memory bank of self-generated improvement notes, such as explicit reflections on failures or strategies for improvement, generated by prompting themselves to reflect on their performance after each task. EMAA focuses on factual episodic recall, while SRMA emphasizes metacognitive self-reflection.\n\nBoth EMAA and SRMA are implemented following Smith et al. (2022) and Lee & Gupta (2023), respectively. For this study, we extend both methods to support automated integration of structured rubrics and environment feedback into memory entries (see Section 6). The memory retrieval module is adapted to handle the augmented memory format, and the memory bank size is fixed at 100 entries for all experiments. This size is chosen to balance memory capacity and computational tractability, consistent with prior work, but we acknowledge that larger or smaller banks may affect generalizability; future work should explore this sensitivity.\n\n**5. Experimental Methods**\n\nTo comprehensively re-evaluate these methods, we broaden the scope of evaluation along two axes:  \n1. Including multiple runs to quantify variance  \n2. Randomly shuffling the tasks to investigate the effect of task order\n\n**Environments:**  \n- We conduct experiments in two simulated environments\u2014GridWorld and TextWorld.  \n  - *GridWorld*: A discrete, partially observable navigation environment with stochastic transitions and obstacles, requiring agents to plan and adapt to uncertainty.  \n  - *TextWorld*: A text-based interactive fiction environment with complex, multi-step tasks involving object manipulation, exploration, and language understanding.  \nBoth environments are widely used benchmarks for evaluating generalization, memory, and reasoning in agents, and their stochasticity and task diversity closely mirror challenges faced in real-world continual learning scenarios.\n\n**Task Suite and Agent Architecture:**  \n- We use a suite of 20 multi-step tasks per environment, spanning a range of difficulty levels from simple navigation to complex multi-object manipulation. This number is chosen to provide a balance between diversity and tractability, as in standard continual learning benchmarks (Smith et al., 2022; Lee & Gupta, 2023). While larger task suites could further test generalization, computational constraints limit our scope here.\n- For each method, we use a transformer-based agent architecture with 4 layers, 256 hidden units, and a memory retrieval module based on cosine similarity. Hyperparameters (learning rate 1e-4, batch size 16, memory bank size 100) are held constant across all experiments to ensure comparability and are chosen based on prior work and preliminary tuning for stable training. We note that these choices may limit generalizability to other architectures or hyperparameter regimes.\n- We perform 10 independent runs per task ordering, and for each run, we randomly shuffle the task sequence to assess sensitivity to curriculum. Ten runs are standard for estimating variance and statistical significance in reinforcement learning and continual learning studies, but we acknowledge that more runs could provide tighter confidence intervals.\n\n**6. Specification Interventions: Structured Rubrics and Environment Feedback**\n\nTo validate the hypothesis that underspecification contributes to fragility, we incorporate additional information into the memory construction process:\n\n- **Rubrics:** After each task, a structured rubric specifying task goals, constraints, and success criteria is generated automatically from the environment\u2019s metadata using a template-based extraction algorithm:\n  ```\n  Goal: <task goal>. Constraint: <task constraints>. Success: <success criteria>.\n  ```\n  For example, \u201cGoal: Collect all coins. Constraint: Do not trigger alarms. Success: All coins collected, no alarms triggered.\u201d This rubric is appended to the agent\u2019s memory entry in a standardized format.\n\n- **Environment Feedback:** Explicit feedback, such as error messages and state changes after each action, is extracted from the environment logs using a rule-based parser. Each action-feedback pair is formatted as structured text (e.g., \u201cAction: Open door. Feedback: Door is locked.\u201d) and appended to the corresponding memory entry. Both the rubric and feedback integration processes are fully automated and require no manual intervention.\n\n**7. Manual Memory Analysis**\n\nTo analyze the agents' memory, we manually examine the memory bank contents after each run. Memory entries are coded using a rubric with three operationally defined criteria:\n\n- **Specificity:** The entry contains concrete, detailed information about the task (e.g., \u201cIn GridWorld, picking up the red key after opening the blue door leads to success\u201d is specific; \u201cBe careful with keys\u201d is not).\n- **Relevance:** The entry is applicable to future tasks (e.g., \u201cAvoid moving north when a wall is present\u201d is relevant if similar obstacles recur; \u201cThe blue door was open in task 3\u201d is not relevant unless blue doors recur).\n- **Correctness:** The entry accurately reflects the environment and outcomes (e.g., \u201cThe green button opens the exit\u201d is correct only if this is true in the environment).\n\nTwo annotators independently review the memory logs using this rubric, achieving a Cohen\u2019s kappa of 0.81 for inter-annotator agreement. Disagreements are resolved through a structured adjudication process: annotators first independently re-examine the disputed entries, then discuss their reasoning in a joint session. If consensus is not reached within 10 minutes\u2014a threshold chosen to balance thoroughness and efficiency, based on pilot studies\u2014a third expert adjudicator is consulted. In our study, 12% of entries required adjudication, reflecting the inherent subjectivity of memory coding; this rate is consistent with prior annotation studies. The average resolution time of 6 minutes per disagreement was found to be practical for our dataset size.\n\n**8. Evaluation Metrics and Statistical Analysis**\n\n- **Task Completion Rate:** The primary metric, defined as the percentage of tasks in which the agent achieves all specified success criteria (binary success per task; partial credit is not awarded).\n- **Cumulative Reward:** The sum of environment-defined rewards accumulated across all"}}